# firewall3: counters
#
# `counter` is a statement that counts packets and bytes of every packet that
# reaches it. `nft list ruleset` then shows `counter packets 12 bytes 720`.
# Great for "is anybody still using telnet before I switch it off?".
#
# The check loads this into b, makes a few telnet (port 23) connection
# attempts from a, then reads the counters with `nft -j list ruleset`. It
# wants the attempts dropped AND counted.
#
# Your task: the counter below stays at 0 forever. Why? (Hint: statements run
# left to right, rules top to bottom, and a verdict ends it all.)

flush ruleset

table inet filter {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority filter; policy accept;
    tcp dport 23 drop
    tcp dport 23 counter comment "telnet attempts"
  }
}
